# Explicit Access Modifiers on Methods

Every method in a class must declare an access modifier: `public`, `private`, or `protected`.

Do **not** rely on package-private (default) visibility by omitting the modifier.

## Do this

```java
// ✅ GOOD — helper on a utility/final class
private static Location resolve(CultureMap cultureMap, CultureZone zone, UUID excludeResidentId) {
    // ...
}

// ✅ GOOD — API entry point
public void scheduleFind(Resident resident, SettlementStatPreference preference) {
    // ...
}

// ✅ GOOD — subclass hook
protected void onCultureZoneReleased(CultureZone released) {
    // ...
}
```

## Do not do this

```java
// ❌ BAD — package-private (missing modifier)
static Location resolve(CultureMap cultureMap, CultureZone zone, UUID excludeResidentId) {
    // ...
}

// ❌ BAD
void finish() {
    // ...
}
```

## Choosing visibility

| Modifier | Use when |
|----------|----------|
| `private` | Internal to the class; helpers, callbacks, task logic |
| `protected` | Shared with subclasses in the same hierarchy |
| `public` | Part of the type’s external API (commands, managers, listeners wired from outside) |

Prefer the **most restrictive** modifier that still works. Do not use `public` for helpers only called inside the same class.

## Agent behavior

When adding or editing Java methods, always include `public`, `private`, or `protected`. If you see a method without a modifier in a file you touch, add the correct one.
